        <title type="html"><![CDATA[FF148 CSS overflow, overflow-x, overflow-y support replaced elements - Relnote/docs]]></title>
        <id>https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/43075</id>
        <link href="https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/43075"/>
        <updated>2026-02-10T21:35:30.000Z</updated>
        <content type="html"><![CDATA[<img src="https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5368500?v=4" width="64" height="64" alt=""/><br/><p dir="auto">The <code class="notranslate">overflow</code>, <code class="notranslate">overflow-x</code>, and <code class="notranslate">overflow-y</code> prooperties set how content that overflows its bounding container should be rendered. Such as by overflowing (visible), hiding or clipping to the bounding container, or clipping and adding scrollbars. Prior to CSS Module 4 this was ignored for replaced elements (such as images) that overflow their container, so they always clipped.<br>
In CSS Module 4 replaced elements do as other elements: <a href="https://drafts.csswg.org/css-overflow-4/#overflow-control" rel="nofollow">https://drafts.csswg.org/css-overflow-4/#overflow-control</a></p>
<p dir="auto">This adds a release note, and also adds a note about replaced elements to the three properties.</p>
<p dir="auto">Note that I chose not to add examples - I figure that going forward most browsers will treat replaced elements in the same way as other elements (though Safari doesn't yet) so this is not a "gotcha" that will matter less and less.</p>
<p dir="auto">Related docs can be tracked in <a class="issue-link js-issue-link" data-error-text="Failed to load title" data-id="3805373106" data-permission-text="Title is private" data-url="https://github.com/mdn/content/issues/42744" data-hovercard-type="issue" data-hovercard-url="/mdn/content/issues/42744/hovercard" href="https://github.com/mdn/content/issues/42744">#42744</a></p>]]></content>
